ue_field_values":[],"type":null,"active_lock_reason":null,"sub_issues_summary":{"total":0,"completed":0,"percent_completed":0},"issue_dependencies_summary":{"blocked_by":0,"total_blocked_by":0,"blocking":0,"total_blocking":0},"body":"### Tested versions\n\nGodot 4.4 rc2\n\n### System information\n\nGodot v4.4.rc2 - Windows 10 (build 19045) - Multi-window, 1 monitor - Vulkan (Forward+) - dedicated Radeon RX 560 Series (Advanced Micro Devices, Inc.; 31.0.21912.14) - Intel(R) Core(TM) i5-4570 CPU @ 3.20GHz (4 threads)\n\n### Issue description\n\nI noticed a strange behavior, the textures we use in shaders, they have a pale gray tint, when using `StandartMaterial3D` I do not observe this problem.\n\nI am new to shaders, and maybe I missed something, sorry if so.\n\nOn the left is a gray cube with a shader, on the right is a standard `StandardMaterial3D`:\n![Image](https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/4e0789a3-a7c5-4a5c-99b4-60008d749649)\n\nI don't see this problem in **Compatibility** mode (Here both textures are even oversaturated, it seems to me):\n\n![Image](https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/fbd37d04-f236-4804-ad54-03f12be1a453)\n\n### Steps to reproduce\n\nAttach this shader to the cube, apply the Godot texture, compare with the standard built-in material, the cube with the shader will be more gray and unsaturated\n\n```\nshader_type spatial;\n\nuniform sampler2D texture;\n\nvoid fragment() {\n    ALBEDO = texture(skin_texture, UV).rgb;\n}\n```\n\n### Minimal reproduction project (MRP)\n\nN/A","closed_by":{"login":"JekSun97","id":130399274,"node_id":"U_kgDOB8W8Kg","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/130399274?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/JekSun97","html_url":"https://github.com/JekSun97","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JekSun97/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JekSun97/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JekSun97/gists{/gist_id}","starre